  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "n1",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "title": "The verb is second",
      "text": "In a Norwegian statement the verb is always the second item. \"Anna er trøtt\" and \"I dag er Anna trøtt\" both have \"er\" second: you do not say \"I dag Anna er trøtt\". \"Er\" is \"is\", \"am\" and \"are\": jeg er, du er, Anna er.",
      "explains": [
        "nb.verb-second",
        "nb.adjective-state"
      ],
      "afterScenes": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "n2",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "title": "Questions start with the verb",
      "text": "\"Anna er trøtt.\" becomes a question by putting the verb first: \"Er Anna trøtt?\" \"Er du trøtt?\". You met the same pattern with \"Vil du ha kaffe?\".",
      "explains": [
        "nb.yes-no-question"
      ],
